THE CHANGE. One file: apps/control-room/src/v2/goals/live-goal-create.ts. 229 -> 275 lines (`wc -l`; note PowerShell `Measure-Object -Line` reports 263 for the same file and UNDERCOUNTS — trust `wc -l`).

WHAT LANDED, four hunks, none of them inside the peer's:
1. `import { readSurfaceOnce } from "../ops/policy-install-port.js";` — the existing helper, imported not copied, per the rail at activation-port.ts:16-19 ("a second fetch idiom for the same route is exactly what this row's rail forbids"). I import the DEFINITION rather than hop through activation-port's re-export, because the goals module has no other reason to depend on the activation port.
2. `export type SurfaceReader = (headers: Readonly<Record<string, string>>) => Promise<SurfaceFrame>;` — the injection seam step 4 needs.
3. `async function rereadSurface(read, headers): Promise<SurfaceFrame | null>` — the re-read, made unable to make things worse.
4. `createGoalDispatcher` gains a third parameter `readSurface: SurfaceReader = readSurfaceOnce` and the offer lookup becomes two-phase.

THE SHAPE, verbatim:
    let frame = getFrame();
    ...
    let offer = goalCreateOffer(frame, kind);
    if (offer === null) {
      // Only ever on a path that was already about to fail: the happy path makes no
      // extra request, because it never reaches here.
      const reread = await rereadSurface(readSurface, setup.headers);
      if (reread !== null) {
        frame = reread;
        offer = goalCreateOffer(reread, kind);
      }
      if (offer === null) return { ok: false, report: goalCreateRefusal(frame, kind) };
    }
`frame` is REASSIGNED, not shadowed, so the refusal on the last line is built from whichever frame the lookup used — that single variable IS the invariant, which is why it is not two separate names.

THE HAPPY PATH PAYS NOTHING. The re-read is inside `if (offer === null)`. A create that had an offer never enters the branch, so it issues zero extra requests. Step 5's adversarial pass re-checks this against the diff.

FAILURE SHAPES HANDLED — DoD-4's degradation rule, and there are THREE, not the two the plan named. `readSurfaceOnce` = `fetch("/affordances/read", { ..., signal: AbortSignal.timeout(15_000) })` then `frameOfSurface(await response.json())`:
 (i) THE FETCH REJECTS — network fault, or the 15 s deadline fires. Caught by `try/catch`, returns null.
 (ii) THE BODY IS NOT A SURFACE — `frameOfSurface` does NOT throw on a bad body; I read it (live-board-feed.ts:259-300) and it returns `unreadable()` = `frame("LAGGING", "UNREADABLE", ...)`, or on a daemon REFUSED outcome `frame("LAGGING", outcome, refusalDetail(...))`. So a catch alone would MISS this one and silently adopt a LAGGING frame.
 (iii) A READABLE BUT NOT-CONNECTED frame, same family as (ii).
The guard `frame.outcome === "SURFACE" && frame.connection === "CONNECTED"` covers (ii) and (iii); the `catch` covers (i). Both degrade to null, and the caller keeps the frame it already polled. That matters for message quality, not just for not-crashing: `goalCreateRefusal` branches on `frame.connection !== "CONNECTED"` FIRST, so adopting a LAGGING re-read would have replaced the true prerequisite sentence ("goal.create_with_source is blocked until Activate the project commits...") with a connectivity sentence ("the board is not connected to the daemon"), i.e. a WORSE report than before the fix. The guard is what makes "worst case unchanged" actually true. Arm (3) in step 4 pins it. Verified against production: a good read returns `frame("CONNECTED", outcome, "", ...)` at live-board-feed.ts:296-299, so the guard admits exactly the readable case.

THE "OLDER FRAME" RACE (planning risk 8), answered honestly rather than guessed. I read `SurfaceFrame` (live-board-feed.ts:34-60) field by field: connection, detail, offers, outcome, planningGoalRef, planningGoalRefs, steps. There is NO version and NO timestamp, so a content comparison is IMPOSSIBLE and any code claiming to "prefer the newer" by inspecting the frames would be inventing an ordering it cannot observe. The ordering the code does have is by REQUEST: `rereadSurface`'s fetch is issued strictly after the poll that wrote the cached frame, so its answer is the daemon's later reading of an aggregate whose committed steps only move forward. That reasoning is written into the `rereadSurface` docblock so the next reader does not "improve" it into a fake version compare.

DoD-4 — THE COMMENT. The old two lines were:
    // ONE read of the frame: the refusal must describe the same surface the offer
    // was looked for on, not a later poll's.
